1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,440
In every castle of the North,
there is a godswood.

2
00:00:03,830 --> 00:00:07,130
And at its heart stands a tree
with bark as white as bone,

3
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:11,730
leaves as red as blood, and a long,
sad face carved into its trunk.

4
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:14,110
The Starks pray before it.

5
00:00:14,510 --> 00:00:16,680
But no Stark ever carved it.

6
00:00:17,150 --> 00:00:19,520
Long before the first
king, before the Wall,

7
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:21,720
before a single man set foot in Westeros,

8
00:00:21,870 --> 00:00:23,260
someone else lived here.

9
00:00:23,770 --> 00:00:26,300
Small, and quick, and dappled like a deer,

10
00:00:26,370 --> 00:00:28,590
with eyes like a cat's.

11
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:29,990
They sang to the trees.

12
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:33,610
And when men came, they broke
the world to stop them.

13
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:36,330
So who was here before the First Men?

14
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:45,890
In the first book, Bran Stark
lies broken in Winterfell.

15
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:49,600
He has fallen from a tower, and
he will never walk again.

16
00:00:50,450 --> 00:00:53,700
To pass the long days, Maester
Luwin gives him lessons.

17
00:00:54,550 --> 00:00:57,680
One afternoon, the lesson is about
the children of the forest.

18
00:00:58,250 --> 00:01:01,000
"They were a people of the Dawn
Age," the maester says,

19
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:05,000
"the very first, before kings
and kingdoms." No castles.

20
00:01:05,610 --> 00:01:06,280
No cities.

21
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:07,790
No men at all.

22
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:12,090
Only the children, in the lands
we now call the Seven Kingdoms.

23
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:15,990
Bran asks if they are still out there.

24
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:18,450
The maester says they are gone.

25
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:22,170
But the wildling woman Osha,
a prisoner in the castle,

26
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:23,590
says something else.

27
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:28,370
She says they still live, beyond the Wall.

28
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,630
One of them was lying.

29
00:01:33,230 --> 00:01:35,580
The books describe them in careful detail.

30
00:01:36,010 --> 00:01:38,980
They were small, about the
size of human children,

31
00:01:39,110 --> 00:01:40,780
but they were not childlike.

32
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:45,370
Their skin was nut-brown, dappled
with paler spots like a deer's.

33
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:47,140
Their ears were large.

34
00:01:47,570 --> 00:01:50,840
Their eyes were gold and green,
slitted like a cat's,

35
00:01:50,870 --> 00:01:52,460
made for seeing in the dark.

36
00:01:52,950 --> 00:01:55,900
And their hands had only three
fingers and a thumb,

37
00:01:55,950 --> 00:01:58,440
with sharp black claws instead of nails.

38
00:01:59,050 --> 00:02:01,560
They had no cities, no metal, no writing.

39
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:06,210
They lived in caves, in crannogs, and
in hidden villages in the trees.

40
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:12,130
Men and women hunted side by side, with
bows of weirwood and blades of black

41
00:02:11,980 --> 00:02:13,190
volcanic glass.

42
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:14,390
Obsidian.

43
00:02:14,790 --> 00:02:16,960
The glass that, thousands of years later,

44
00:02:17,110 --> 00:02:18,720
men would call dragonglass.

45
00:02:19,330 --> 00:02:21,440
Their gods were the trees themselves.

46
00:02:21,790 --> 00:02:26,090
They carved faces into the weirwoods, so
the gods could watch over the forest.

47
00:02:26,520 --> 00:02:30,310
And a rare few were born with
eyes as green as moss,

48
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:32,010
or red as blood.

49
00:02:32,410 --> 00:02:33,520
The greenseers.

50
00:02:34,070 --> 00:02:36,540
Through the weirwoods, they
could see far away,

51
00:02:36,390 --> 00:02:37,620
and long ago.

52
00:02:38,330 --> 00:02:39,560
They were not alone.

53
00:02:40,070 --> 00:02:43,700
The giants lived here too,
ten to fourteen feet tall,

54
00:02:44,070 --> 00:02:46,180
shaggy with fur, riding mammoths.

55
00:02:46,690 --> 00:02:49,280
The two peoples did not always get along.

56
00:02:49,850 --> 00:02:52,960
The maesters record a giant's
grave in the North,

57
00:02:52,810 --> 00:02:56,380
with obsidian arrowheads
still lodged in its ribs.

58
00:02:57,130 --> 00:03:00,500
For thousands of years, this
was the whole world.

59
00:03:01,550 --> 00:03:04,140
Then, from the east, came men.

60
00:03:06,740 --> 00:03:11,270
In those days, Westeros and the eastern
continent were joined by a bridge

61
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:13,210
of land, the Arm of Dorne.

62
00:03:13,860 --> 00:03:15,350
Across it came the First Men.

63
00:03:15,820 --> 00:03:18,030
The maesters cannot agree when.

64
00:03:18,430 --> 00:03:20,440
Some say twelve thousand years ago.

65
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:21,930
Others say eight.

66
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:25,670
They carried bronze swords
and great leather shields.

67
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:29,240
They rode horses, animals the
children had never seen.

68
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:31,290
And they brought their own gods.

69
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:35,090
At first, the histories say,
the children welcomed them.

70
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:38,830
But the First Men cleared the
forests for their fields.

71
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:42,040
Then they began to fear
the faces in the trees.

72
00:03:42,530 --> 00:03:45,040
They believed the children
used them to spy.

73
00:03:45,530 --> 00:03:48,540
So they burned the weirwoods,
and cut them down.

74
00:03:49,410 --> 00:03:51,360
And the children went to war.

75
00:03:52,651 --> 00:03:56,301
What the children did next changed
the shape of the continent.

76
00:03:57,211 --> 00:03:59,981
If you want Westeros the
way it is in the books,

77
00:04:00,331 --> 00:04:01,961
subscribe to the Mythic Archives.

78
00:04:05,161 --> 00:04:06,931
The war lasted for centuries.

79
00:04:07,331 --> 00:04:09,941
The children had weirwood
bows and obsidian.

80
00:04:10,311 --> 00:04:12,611
The First Men had bronze, and numbers.

81
00:04:13,161 --> 00:04:17,151
In the riverlands, one legendary warrior
earned the name Brandon of the

82
00:04:17,001 --> 00:04:17,751
Bloody Blade.

83
00:04:18,541 --> 00:04:20,051
The children were losing.

84
00:04:20,621 --> 00:04:23,551
So their greenseers turned to
the darkest magic they had.

85
00:04:24,441 --> 00:04:27,771
Legend says they gathered and called
down the hammer of the waters.

86
00:04:28,171 --> 00:04:28,981
The sea rose.

87
00:04:29,451 --> 00:04:30,421
The land shook.

88
00:04:30,821 --> 00:04:33,071
And the Arm of Dorne broke apart.

89
00:04:33,681 --> 00:04:36,251
Where the bridge had stood,
there was only water,

90
00:04:36,101 --> 00:04:40,291
and a broken chain of islands that
we now call the Stepstones.

91
00:04:41,281 --> 00:04:42,451
It was too late.

92
00:04:43,461 --> 00:04:46,771
The door was closed, but the First
Men were already through.

93
00:04:47,601 --> 00:04:49,931
Legend says the greenseers
tried once more,

94
00:04:50,021 --> 00:04:53,311
in the North, at the narrow neck
of land between the swamps.

95
00:04:54,001 --> 00:04:56,611
At the ancient fortress of Moat Cailin,

96
00:04:56,961 --> 00:05:00,811
one ruined tower still bears their
name: the Children's Tower,

97
00:05:00,901 --> 00:05:03,991
where they called upon their gods
to bring the hammer down again.

98
00:05:04,401 --> 00:05:08,991
Some say that is why the Neck is
drowned and full of bogs today.

99
00:05:10,381 --> 00:05:11,611
But the Neck held.

100
00:05:12,421 --> 00:05:14,531
And the war went on.

101
00:05:17,131 --> 00:05:19,201
At last, both sides grew weary.

102
00:05:19,771 --> 00:05:22,081
In the middle of a great
lake in the riverlands,

103
00:05:22,091 --> 00:05:24,741
called the Gods Eye, there
is a small island.

104
00:05:25,591 --> 00:05:29,481
There, the greenseers and wood dancers
of the children met the chiefs and

105
00:05:29,331 --> 00:05:31,961
heroes of the First Men,
and made the Pact.

106
00:05:32,491 --> 00:05:36,561
The First Men would take the coasts,
the high plains and bright meadows,

107
00:05:36,771 --> 00:05:38,201
the mountains and the bogs.

108
00:05:38,551 --> 00:05:41,211
The deep woods would belong
to the children forever.

109
00:05:41,721 --> 00:05:44,311
And no more weirwoods would
be put to the axe,

110
00:05:44,561 --> 00:05:45,611
anywhere in the realm.

111
00:05:46,221 --> 00:05:50,971
So that the gods could witness it, a
face was carved into every tree on

112
00:05:50,821 --> 00:05:51,431
the island.

113
00:05:51,941 --> 00:05:54,571
From that day it was called
the Isle of Faces.

114
00:05:55,541 --> 00:05:58,941
A secret order, the green men,
was formed to guard it.

115
00:05:59,311 --> 00:06:01,701
In the books, some say
they guard it still.

116
00:06:02,591 --> 00:06:05,781
What followed were four thousand
years of friendship.

117
00:06:06,351 --> 00:06:07,761
The Age of Heroes.

118
00:06:08,371 --> 00:06:12,941
In time, the First Men put aside the
gods they had brought with them,

119
00:06:12,791 --> 00:06:15,421
and worshipped the old gods of the forest.

120
00:06:15,851 --> 00:06:20,461
When the Long Night fell, men and
children fought side by side.

121
00:06:21,011 --> 00:06:25,101
And according to the Night's Watch,
the children once gave the brothers

122
00:06:24,951 --> 00:06:27,961
a hundred obsidian daggers every year.

123
00:06:30,561 --> 00:06:34,731
Then, the books say, a new people
crossed the narrow sea.

124
00:06:35,181 --> 00:06:36,051
The Andals.

125
00:06:36,701 --> 00:06:38,111
They had iron and steel.

126
00:06:38,511 --> 00:06:43,241
And they had a new faith, the Seven,
with seven-pointed stars carved into

127
00:06:43,091 --> 00:06:44,081
their own chests.

128
00:06:44,491 --> 00:06:47,761
To the Andals, the faces
in the trees were idols.

129
00:06:48,161 --> 00:06:51,411
They cut down the weirwoods, hunted
the children who were left,

130
00:06:51,621 --> 00:06:53,931
and swept away the kingdoms
of the First Men,

131
00:06:54,221 --> 00:06:55,051
one by one.

132
00:06:55,721 --> 00:06:58,791
In the riverlands, there is
a hill called High Heart.

133
00:06:59,321 --> 00:07:02,211
Around its crown stand thirty-one
white stumps,

134
00:07:02,421 --> 00:07:04,991
the remains of a sacred weirwood grove.

135
00:07:05,391 --> 00:07:09,581
The songs say an Andal king killed the
children there and cut the grove

136
00:07:09,431 --> 00:07:09,861
down.

137
00:07:10,261 --> 00:07:13,191
The smallfolk say the hill is
still haunted by their ghosts.

138
00:07:13,621 --> 00:07:15,451
But one kingdom never fell.

139
00:07:15,921 --> 00:07:20,051
At Moat Cailin, the Andals were broken
against the ruins and the bogs.

140
00:07:20,451 --> 00:07:23,131
The North stayed a land of the First Men.

141
00:07:23,541 --> 00:07:28,071
That is why, thousands of years later,
the Starks still pray before a heart

142
00:07:27,921 --> 00:07:30,171
tree, and not in a sept.

143
00:07:32,770 --> 00:07:37,380
On television, the children of the forest
have skin like bark and leaves

144
00:07:37,230 --> 00:07:38,840
growing from their bodies.

145
00:07:39,370 --> 00:07:43,460
And in one scene, they bind
a First Man to a weirwood,

146
00:07:43,310 --> 00:07:46,700
and drive a blade of dragonglass
into his heart.

147
00:07:47,190 --> 00:07:49,860
He becomes the first White Walker.

148
00:07:50,770 --> 00:07:55,440
The children, the show says,
created their own destroyer.

149
00:07:56,450 --> 00:07:57,760
None of that is in the books.

150
00:07:58,610 --> 00:08:01,840
In the books, the children are not plants.

151
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:05,830
They are small, dappled, golden-eyed
hunters with claws.

152
00:08:06,230 --> 00:08:10,000
And the books never say where
the Others came from.

153
00:08:10,610 --> 00:08:12,640
Nothing in them blames the children.

154
00:08:13,430 --> 00:08:18,580
In the books, the children were the ones
who stood beside men when the cold

155
00:08:18,430 --> 00:08:18,960
came.

156
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:24,370
Beyond the Wall, in the
last published novel,

157
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:25,970
Bran finally finds them.

158
00:08:26,220 --> 00:08:28,400
They save him from the dead in the snow,

159
00:08:28,250 --> 00:08:31,340
and carry him into a cave
beneath a hollow hill.

160
00:08:32,010 --> 00:08:33,200
One of them is called Leaf.

161
00:08:33,850 --> 00:08:36,980
She tells him that "children"
is only what men call them.

162
00:08:37,380 --> 00:08:39,650
The giants call them the
little squirrel people.

163
00:08:40,050 --> 00:08:43,380
Their own name is older: those
who sing the song of earth.

164
00:08:44,030 --> 00:08:46,340
"Before your Old Tongue
was spoken," she says,

165
00:08:46,590 --> 00:08:51,760
"we had been singing our songs for ten
thousand years." Deep in the cave,

166
00:08:51,610 --> 00:08:55,340
Bran meets the last greenseer:
a pale, withered man,

167
00:08:55,810 --> 00:08:58,020
half swallowed by the roots of a weirwood,

168
00:08:58,230 --> 00:09:00,740
his one red eye glowing in the dark.

169
00:09:01,430 --> 00:09:03,980
Through the trees, Bran
begins to see the past.

170
00:09:04,630 --> 00:09:09,100
In one vision, he watches the First
Men offer blood to the heart tree of

171
00:09:08,950 --> 00:09:09,600
Winterfell.

172
00:09:10,230 --> 00:09:11,920
He tastes it in his mouth.

173
00:09:12,410 --> 00:09:13,740
Where are the rest of them?

174
00:09:14,370 --> 00:09:17,380
Leaf answers that the singers
have gone into the earth,

175
00:09:17,570 --> 00:09:19,580
into the stones, and into the trees.

176
00:09:19,980 --> 00:09:24,130
"That was in the dawn of days, when
our sun was rising," she says.

177
00:09:24,780 --> 00:09:30,570
"Now it sinks, and this is our long dwindling."
The giants are almost gone

178
00:09:30,420 --> 00:09:30,930
too.

179
00:09:31,340 --> 00:09:33,330
The mammoths are down to a few hundred.

180
00:09:33,820 --> 00:09:36,450
"In the world that men
have made," she says,

181
00:09:36,740 --> 00:09:41,570
"there is no room for them, or us."
But the old weapons still work.

182
00:09:42,360 --> 00:09:47,190
On a hill beyond the Wall, the Night's Watch
finds a buried bundle of dragonglass.

183
00:09:48,140 --> 00:09:53,470
Months later, a frightened young brother
named Samwell Tarly drives a dragonglass

184
00:09:53,320 --> 00:09:57,110
dagger into one of the Others,
and it melts away.

185
00:09:57,920 --> 00:10:04,190
The children's glass, waiting in the
ground for eight thousand years.

186
00:10:05,590 --> 00:10:08,240
So, who was here before the First Men?

187
00:10:08,730 --> 00:10:12,100
A small, singing people who carved
the gods into the trees,

188
00:10:11,950 --> 00:10:14,840
and broke the land itself to defend them.

189
00:10:15,190 --> 00:10:16,810
They lost their world.

190
00:10:17,520 --> 00:10:21,130
But they kept their bargain, and
taught the newcomers to pray.

191
00:10:22,300 --> 00:10:25,650
Were the First Men invaders
who stole a world,

192
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:28,430
or did the Pact make them its guardians?

193
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:31,530
Tell us what you think in the comments.

194
00:10:31,380 --> 00:10:34,980
Like this video if the
books surprised you,

195
00:10:35,010 --> 00:10:37,080
and subscribe to the Mythic Archives.

196
00:10:37,490 --> 00:10:39,360
The archive is always open.
